WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:09.150
HADLEY WICKHAM: So I am
really so thrilled

00:00:09.150 --> 00:00:15.840
to introduce our next keynote
speakers, Martin Wattenberg

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:21.390
and Fernanda Viegas, because
Martin and Fernando, I believe,

00:00:21.390 --> 00:00:23.490
are really two of
the top visualization

00:00:23.490 --> 00:00:25.950
researchers in the world today.

00:00:25.950 --> 00:00:29.670
I have been a follower of
their work for over 10 years.

00:00:29.670 --> 00:00:32.438
Chances are you have seen
some of their work before.

00:00:32.438 --> 00:00:34.230
In fact, if you've ever
looked up

00:00:34.230 --> 00:00:35.965
the definition of
a word on Google

00:00:35.965 --> 00:00:37.590
and seen a little
line chart, I believe

00:00:37.590 --> 00:00:41.090
that is Martin's most
popular, or most well-seen

00:00:41.090 --> 00:00:42.570
visualization.

00:00:42.570 --> 00:00:45.300
One of my personal
favorites is the wind map--

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:51.390
really beautiful
visualization of dynamic

00:00:51.390 --> 00:00:53.370
wind currents over
the continental US.

00:00:53.370 --> 00:00:55.590
If you google wind map--
fantastic, fantastic

00:00:55.590 --> 00:00:57.360
visualization.

00:00:57.360 --> 00:00:59.340
But recently,
Fernanda and Martin

00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:02.130
have been working on
using visualization

00:01:02.130 --> 00:01:05.099
to display really
complex ideas and

00:01:05.099 --> 00:01:08.490
unpack many of the tools
of modern machine learning

00:01:08.490 --> 00:01:10.230
and artificial intelligence.

00:01:10.230 --> 00:01:14.950
So please join me in
welcoming Fernanda and Martin.

00:01:14.950 --> 00:01:16.210
FERNANDA VIEGAS: Thank you.

00:01:16.210 --> 00:01:17.918
MARTIN WATTERNBERG:
Thank you, thank you.

00:01:22.110 --> 00:01:25.842
So yeah, thank you so much.

00:01:25.842 --> 00:01:28.050
Want to talk a little bit
about what we're doing now,

00:01:28.050 --> 00:01:30.670
because it combines
both the visualization--

00:01:30.670 --> 00:01:32.910
which has been our bread
and butter for a while--

00:01:32.910 --> 00:01:34.140
with AI.

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:39.270
We are co-leads with Jeff
Holbrook of a group at Google

00:01:39.270 --> 00:01:40.350
called PAIR--

00:01:40.350 --> 00:01:43.110
stands for People
plus AI Research.

00:01:43.110 --> 00:01:45.690
And before saying
anything more about it,

00:01:45.690 --> 00:01:48.750
I want to quickly put up
pictures of the people

00:01:48.750 --> 00:01:51.750
in the team, because I want to
emphasize that everything we're

00:01:51.750 --> 00:01:54.740
talking about today-- this was
all very much a team effort,

00:01:54.740 --> 00:01:58.920
and these people deserve credit.

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:03.360
The mission of PAIR is
to make machine learning

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:05.640
and artificial intelligence
productive, enjoyable,

00:02:05.640 --> 00:02:07.440
and fair.

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:09.539
And we do that in various
ways, and one of which--

00:02:09.539 --> 00:02:10.914
I'm happy to say,
actually, we've

00:02:10.914 --> 00:02:13.860
released a fair number of
open-source tools, which

00:02:13.860 --> 00:02:15.630
we'll talk about today.

00:02:15.630 --> 00:02:18.090
We also create
educational materials,

00:02:18.090 --> 00:02:22.560
academic publications, and do
a lot of public work as well.

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:26.070
Now, you might ask, why are
we talking about human-AI

00:02:26.070 --> 00:02:28.770
interaction at this conference?

00:02:28.770 --> 00:02:31.500
And there's sort of a snarky
answer you could give,

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:35.750
which is that some might say
is just fancy statistics.

00:02:35.750 --> 00:02:38.010
And maybe there's
some truth to that.

00:02:38.010 --> 00:02:41.790
But there's a more
serious answer,

00:02:41.790 --> 00:02:45.000
which is that you, as
people who analyze data,

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:47.090
you have a lot of power,
and you know that.

00:02:47.090 --> 00:02:50.340
But you might have even more
power than you realize--

00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:54.460
that, as AI becomes more
and more pervasive,

00:02:54.460 --> 00:02:56.640
it's turning out
the data analysis

00:02:56.640 --> 00:03:01.890
is very much the key
to getting AI right.

00:03:01.890 --> 00:03:05.280
FERNANDA VIEGAS:
So to start with, we're going
to start at the beginning.

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:07.990
We have a few projects we'd
like to share with you,

00:03:07.990 --> 00:03:12.930
and one of them is about just
visualizing training data.

00:03:12.930 --> 00:03:17.190
I'm sure you all know this,
that a big part of what

00:03:17.190 --> 00:03:21.450
goes into these massive machine
learning systems is the data.

00:03:21.450 --> 00:03:26.040
And you need to be super careful
and mindful about the data.

00:03:26.040 --> 00:03:30.150
And yet, when we talk with
engineers-- software engineers

00:03:30.150 --> 00:03:33.420
about some of the problems
that come up in these systems,

00:03:33.420 --> 00:03:38.520
their first instinct tends
to be to debug their program.

00:03:38.520 --> 00:03:41.430
And so our favorite motto
is, debug your data first,

00:03:41.430 --> 00:03:43.140
not your program.

00:03:43.140 --> 00:03:45.390
And this is something
of a learning curve.

00:03:45.390 --> 00:03:49.500
And I think you all are
way ahead of the engineers,

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:52.890
but one of the realities
is that we need tools

00:03:52.890 --> 00:03:56.280
for dealing with the
huge amounts of data

00:03:56.280 --> 00:03:59.770
that these people are using.

00:03:59.770 --> 00:04:01.500
And so we're still
working on that.

00:04:01.500 --> 00:04:04.090
One example of a
tool we created,

00:04:04.090 --> 00:04:08.610
which is a visualization
for just training data--

00:04:08.610 --> 00:04:12.150
I'm going to demo that
with one of the hello world

00:04:12.150 --> 00:04:16.230
data sets of machine learning,
which is called CIFAR-10.

00:04:16.230 --> 00:04:18.990
And all it is-- it's
a bunch of images,

00:04:18.990 --> 00:04:24.810
squared little images of 10
different classes of things.

00:04:24.810 --> 00:04:30.130
Each image has just one main
object or entity inside of it.

00:04:30.130 --> 00:04:33.300
And the entire data set
has been human labeled

00:04:33.300 --> 00:04:35.560
in these 10 different classes.

00:04:35.560 --> 00:04:39.960
So you have deer, you have
a car, you have automobile,

00:04:39.960 --> 00:04:42.090
and so forth.

00:04:42.090 --> 00:04:46.230
And another thing that's
important about this data set

00:04:46.230 --> 00:04:48.840
is that, because it is one
of the hello world data

00:04:48.840 --> 00:04:51.720
sets of machine learning,
it is used everywhere.

00:04:51.720 --> 00:04:53.160
It is used as a benchmark.

00:04:53.160 --> 00:04:57.300
It's been used by thousands
of people all over the world,

00:04:57.300 --> 00:05:01.860
and it's used in
publications as well.

00:05:01.860 --> 00:05:03.120
How do I get--

00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:08.390
Maybe that X.

00:05:08.390 --> 00:05:09.990
Let's get to the demo.

00:05:09.990 --> 00:05:11.810
OK, cool.

00:05:11.810 --> 00:05:14.720
So this is our
little visualization,

00:05:14.720 --> 00:05:16.910
and it's called Facets.

00:05:16.910 --> 00:05:20.720
And all I'm doing
here is visualizing

00:05:20.720 --> 00:05:23.810
the images I have just
by the different classes.

00:05:23.810 --> 00:05:25.910
So as far as data
visualization goes,

00:05:25.910 --> 00:05:28.730
it's extremely straightforward.

00:05:28.730 --> 00:05:30.260
I can zoom in.

00:05:30.260 --> 00:05:34.190
I can look at
images very closely,

00:05:34.190 --> 00:05:37.070
if I want to, see how
pixilated they are, and so

00:05:37.070 --> 00:05:39.330
forth-- nothing super special.

00:05:39.330 --> 00:05:41.450
You can see the
labels of the classes,

00:05:41.450 --> 00:05:43.670
and you can also see
that, if I accidentally

00:05:43.670 --> 00:05:47.030
click on any one of the
images, it brings up

00:05:47.030 --> 00:05:49.430
a little card at
the very top there

00:05:49.430 --> 00:05:55.130
with the metadata about
each one of these images.

00:05:55.130 --> 00:05:57.410
So far, nothing special--

00:05:57.410 --> 00:06:01.430
but even this is hard
for regular engineers

00:06:01.430 --> 00:06:04.490
using machine learning
systems to get to see.

00:06:04.490 --> 00:06:07.250
They don't usually get to
see their training data.

00:06:07.250 --> 00:06:08.360
And the reason why--

00:06:08.360 --> 00:06:10.773
I'm sure you all realize--

00:06:10.773 --> 00:06:13.190
why you want to do something
like this is because it could

00:06:13.190 --> 00:06:15.850
be that half of your
data set is just empty,

00:06:15.850 --> 00:06:17.840
is just blank images.

00:06:17.840 --> 00:06:21.740
Or even worse than that,
it could be the just one

00:06:21.740 --> 00:06:25.320
of your classes is
blank, and nothing else.

00:06:25.320 --> 00:06:29.090
And so you want to be able
to just very quickly play

00:06:29.090 --> 00:06:31.310
with your data and look at it.

00:06:31.310 --> 00:06:33.890
Once we have this, we
can play little games.

00:06:33.890 --> 00:06:37.010
So I can look at the
same visualization,

00:06:37.010 --> 00:06:40.130
but I can now
distribute it by hue.

00:06:40.130 --> 00:06:42.690
And I can see that there
are different bulges

00:06:42.690 --> 00:06:44.730
in different classes.

00:06:44.730 --> 00:06:47.480
So I can see that
airplane at the very top

00:06:47.480 --> 00:06:50.510
and ship towards the
bottom are the classes

00:06:50.510 --> 00:06:54.500
with the most blue images.

00:06:54.500 --> 00:06:56.960
That kind of makes sense.

00:06:56.960 --> 00:07:00.560
I'm taking photos against
the sky or against the water.

00:07:00.560 --> 00:07:05.330
And then all of my animal
classes in the middle-- bird,

00:07:05.330 --> 00:07:07.370
cat, deer--

00:07:07.370 --> 00:07:10.920
they tend to hang out
more on the earthy tones.

00:07:10.920 --> 00:07:12.980
OK, so far, so good.

00:07:12.980 --> 00:07:14.510
Now, I can do other things.

00:07:14.510 --> 00:07:19.790
I can say, now show
me a confusion matrix.

00:07:19.790 --> 00:07:22.670
A confusion matrix
is basically me

00:07:22.670 --> 00:07:26.990
trying to understand
how in sync humans

00:07:26.990 --> 00:07:30.050
and my machine learning
system really are.

00:07:30.050 --> 00:07:32.990
So everything on
the column there

00:07:32.990 --> 00:07:36.950
are the labels that have been
manually given to these images

00:07:36.950 --> 00:07:39.710
by humans, and
everything at the top

00:07:39.710 --> 00:07:43.160
are the labels that my
system is giving, as it

00:07:43.160 --> 00:07:45.500
tries to classify these images.

00:07:45.500 --> 00:07:48.260
So the good news for
me is that the diagonal

00:07:48.260 --> 00:07:52.730
is by far the most populated
set of cells I have there,

00:07:52.730 --> 00:07:56.060
because that's where
humans and machine agree.

00:07:56.060 --> 00:07:57.920
So that's great.

00:07:57.920 --> 00:08:00.920
But then other things
they can do with this--

00:08:00.920 --> 00:08:03.140
I can say, OK, so
now let's filter out

00:08:03.140 --> 00:08:05.000
all the correct guesses.

00:08:05.000 --> 00:08:08.720
I'm going to filter
out that diagonal.

00:08:08.720 --> 00:08:11.030
And then immediately,
I have all the mistakes

00:08:11.030 --> 00:08:12.740
that my system is making.

00:08:12.740 --> 00:08:15.980
And I can see an
interesting pattern here.

00:08:15.980 --> 00:08:19.790
I can see that my
most populated cell--

00:08:19.790 --> 00:08:21.740
or couple of cells--

00:08:21.740 --> 00:08:24.650
over here and over here--

00:08:24.650 --> 00:08:27.380
is the intersection
between cats and dogs.

00:08:27.380 --> 00:08:31.820
My system is kind of confused
between cats and dogs.

00:08:31.820 --> 00:08:34.460
So it could be that, at
this point, I go back

00:08:34.460 --> 00:08:38.809
and give my system more
examples of cats and dogs

00:08:38.809 --> 00:08:43.549
so we can retrain, and hopefully
get better at these classes.

00:08:43.549 --> 00:08:46.400
This could be one
of the things I do.

00:08:46.400 --> 00:08:49.760
All right, another thing I
can do with this visualization

00:08:49.760 --> 00:08:52.460
is look at the softmax label.

00:08:52.460 --> 00:08:55.980
So towards the
end of my network,

00:08:55.980 --> 00:09:00.740
I want to understand not only
how these images are being

00:09:00.740 --> 00:09:03.720
classified by my
system, but I also

00:09:03.720 --> 00:09:07.550
want to understand
how sure my system is,

00:09:07.550 --> 00:09:12.470
how certain my system is
of any of those classifications

00:09:12.470 --> 00:09:14.010
it makes.

00:09:14.010 --> 00:09:20.280
So the way to read this is, the
more an image is to the right,

00:09:20.280 --> 00:09:23.780
the more certain my system
is that that is indeed

00:09:23.780 --> 00:09:27.140
a dog, or a cat, or an airplane.

00:09:27.140 --> 00:09:33.740
And everything-- the more to the
right, the more correct it is.

00:09:33.740 --> 00:09:38.210
On the contrary, the more
to the left you have--

00:09:38.210 --> 00:09:41.690
these over here-- the
more certain my system

00:09:41.690 --> 00:09:45.740
is that that is not a
cat or not an airplane.

00:09:45.740 --> 00:09:49.880
And those are incorrect
classifications.

00:09:49.880 --> 00:09:52.910
So one of the things
that I want to do here

00:09:52.910 --> 00:09:56.480
is I might want to go-- we
became interested-- we're like,

00:09:56.480 --> 00:09:57.110
oh wow.

00:09:57.110 --> 00:09:58.220
But check it out.

00:09:58.220 --> 00:10:00.680
Cats is still very
populated here.

00:10:00.680 --> 00:10:03.860
There's a bunch of cats my
system is very, very sure

00:10:03.860 --> 00:10:05.300
are not cats.

00:10:05.300 --> 00:10:09.180
So we zoomed in and
started looking like, wow,

00:10:09.180 --> 00:10:12.210
what do these cats look like,
that my system just-- very

00:10:12.210 --> 00:10:15.270
certain aren't cats?

00:10:15.270 --> 00:10:21.110
And again, think back that
this is a benchmark data

00:10:21.110 --> 00:10:26.650
set used by lots of
people all over the world.

00:10:26.650 --> 00:10:30.240
We started looking at
this, and we're like,

00:10:30.240 --> 00:10:34.370
there's totally a thing
there that's not a cat.

00:10:34.370 --> 00:10:40.520
Can anyone spot-- it's on
the second row to the bottom,

00:10:40.520 --> 00:10:43.010
over here.

00:10:43.010 --> 00:10:47.250
My system is very
sure that's a frog.

00:10:47.250 --> 00:10:54.260
And so am I. And
yet, it has been

00:10:54.260 --> 00:10:58.490
labeled by humans as a cat.

00:10:58.490 --> 00:11:02.120
So what is our little
tale telling us here?

00:11:02.120 --> 00:11:03.680
The same tale as usual--

00:11:03.680 --> 00:11:06.140
you have to go back to
your data and look at it.

00:11:06.140 --> 00:11:08.250
And there are going
to be mistakes.

00:11:08.250 --> 00:11:11.720
And if you can't look
at it in an easy way,

00:11:11.720 --> 00:11:15.410
you're going to continue to see
these mistakes over and over

00:11:15.410 --> 00:11:16.750
again.

00:11:16.750 --> 00:11:23.030
So this was a tool that we
started using internally,

00:11:23.030 --> 00:11:25.070
and then decided
this is actually

00:11:25.070 --> 00:11:29.720
a simple, straightforward tool
that's actually super useful.

00:11:29.720 --> 00:11:35.600
And we decided to open-source--
so Facets is a open-source tool

00:11:35.600 --> 00:11:38.060
available for anyone.

00:11:38.060 --> 00:11:43.490
And yes, I know that
you can do this in R.

00:11:43.490 --> 00:11:47.140
And I'm sure that a lot of
you are doing it already--

00:11:47.140 --> 00:11:51.090
slice and dicing-- simple stuff.

00:11:51.090 --> 00:11:53.790
But there is a lot of
people who don't use R too,

00:11:53.790 --> 00:12:00.800
and so having something
on the web is also useful.

00:12:00.800 --> 00:12:03.380
All right, the next thing
we want to talk about

00:12:03.380 --> 00:12:05.990
is this notion
that, as soon as you

00:12:05.990 --> 00:12:09.710
start feeding these massive data
sets to these machine learning

00:12:09.710 --> 00:12:13.670
systems, you are going
to be talking about bias

00:12:13.670 --> 00:12:16.670
and skews in your data set.

00:12:16.670 --> 00:12:20.600
And as soon as you start
deploying these systems

00:12:20.600 --> 00:12:22.550
in the world, you
are going to be

00:12:22.550 --> 00:12:24.860
faced with fairness questions.

00:12:29.130 --> 00:12:31.610
These are very complex issues.

00:12:31.610 --> 00:12:34.190
It's not like you have
a technical solution

00:12:34.190 --> 00:12:37.730
for these things,
but there is also

00:12:37.730 --> 00:12:41.780
a technical piece to trying
to solve some of these things.

00:12:41.780 --> 00:12:46.280
So Martin and I we're
interested in whether we

00:12:46.280 --> 00:12:49.850
could use very simple data
visualization to start

00:12:49.850 --> 00:12:52.340
illustrating some
of the trade-offs

00:12:52.340 --> 00:12:55.670
that you have to make, when you
start thinking about fairness

00:12:55.670 --> 00:12:56.570
in machine learning.

00:12:59.660 --> 00:13:01.120
As I introduce this
visualization--

00:13:01.120 --> 00:13:02.550
it's very simple--

00:13:02.550 --> 00:13:05.540
we're going to play
a little game here,

00:13:05.540 --> 00:13:09.620
which is we are going to
imagine that we are a bank

00:13:09.620 --> 00:13:13.160
and we're deciding who
we're going to give loans to

00:13:13.160 --> 00:13:16.040
and who we're going
to not give loans to.

00:13:16.040 --> 00:13:19.910
So each one of these
dots is a person

00:13:19.910 --> 00:13:23.840
who comes to our bank
asking for a loan.

00:13:23.840 --> 00:13:26.570
They have a credit score.

00:13:26.570 --> 00:13:28.310
All of these things
are imaginary.

00:13:28.310 --> 00:13:32.330
My credit score
goes from 0 to 100.

00:13:32.330 --> 00:13:36.620
And their location is--

00:13:36.620 --> 00:13:38.480
reflects their credit score.

00:13:41.600 --> 00:13:51.350
Each one of the light colored
dots would default on our loan.

00:13:51.350 --> 00:13:54.510
All of the dark colored
dots would pay us back.

00:13:54.510 --> 00:13:56.180
So in other words,
we want to give loans

00:13:56.180 --> 00:13:58.640
to the dark colored
dots, and we do not

00:13:58.640 --> 00:14:01.850
want to give loans to
the light colored dots.

00:14:01.850 --> 00:14:04.610
Many people come to our bank.

00:14:04.610 --> 00:14:06.350
We set a threshold.

00:14:06.350 --> 00:14:08.870
Below that threshold,
we don't give loans.

00:14:08.870 --> 00:14:11.390
Above that threshold,
we give loans.

00:14:11.390 --> 00:14:12.910
So far, so good--

00:14:12.910 --> 00:14:18.020
except that, in reality,
life is not that simple.

00:14:18.020 --> 00:14:21.470
So no matter where
I put my threshold,

00:14:21.470 --> 00:14:24.110
chances are I'm going
to be denying loans

00:14:24.110 --> 00:14:27.170
to people who would pay me back,
and I would be giving loans

00:14:27.170 --> 00:14:30.290
to people who default.

00:14:30.290 --> 00:14:33.830
And so this is still
a simplification

00:14:33.830 --> 00:14:35.810
of the real world, but
it's a distribution

00:14:35.810 --> 00:14:39.890
that starts to look a
little bit more realistic.

00:14:39.890 --> 00:14:42.680
And you can see that, no matter
where you put your threshold,

00:14:42.680 --> 00:14:44.675
you're going to make
incorrect guesses.

00:14:47.840 --> 00:14:52.910
Then the next question
becomes, well,

00:14:52.910 --> 00:14:56.450
you may have different
populations of users coming--

00:14:56.450 --> 00:15:00.020
people coming to your
bank and asking for loans.

00:15:00.020 --> 00:15:02.300
And you may know that
these distributions

00:15:02.300 --> 00:15:04.490
are different for
these different people.

00:15:09.140 --> 00:15:11.810
How are you going to be fair?
Now that you have
a blue population

00:15:11.810 --> 00:15:14.720
and an orange population
with different distributions,

00:15:14.720 --> 00:15:19.700
how are you going to
decide how to be fair?

00:15:19.700 --> 00:15:22.740
And there are many different
ways you can do this.

00:15:22.740 --> 00:15:28.460
So let me just show you the
little demo/visualization

00:15:28.460 --> 00:15:31.000
we created.

00:15:31.000 --> 00:15:34.060
This accompanies,
by the way, a paper

00:15:34.060 --> 00:15:38.150
on machine learning fairness.

00:15:38.150 --> 00:15:42.640
So basically, we just created
this little simulation here,

00:15:42.640 --> 00:15:46.240
which is kind of what you
had been seeing before.

00:15:46.240 --> 00:15:49.180
And it's interactive,
and I can play with it.

00:15:49.180 --> 00:15:51.040
And you can see what
happens underneath,

00:15:51.040 --> 00:15:54.550
as I'm changing the
threshold here at the top.

00:15:54.550 --> 00:15:57.670
At the bottom, you
can see the percentage

00:15:57.670 --> 00:16:02.980
of incorrect and
correct guesses I make.

00:16:02.980 --> 00:16:05.200
You can also see
things at the bottom--

00:16:05.200 --> 00:16:08.020
here are these
pie chart things--

00:16:08.020 --> 00:16:14.770
they stand for the positive
rate of loans they give.

00:16:14.770 --> 00:16:16.750
So in other words,
of all the people

00:16:16.750 --> 00:16:19.630
who come to my bank, what
is the percentage of people

00:16:19.630 --> 00:16:21.370
I give loans to?

00:16:21.370 --> 00:16:24.040
And then there is another
pie chart that says--

00:16:24.040 --> 00:16:28.150
sorry-- the positive
rate is on the right,

00:16:28.150 --> 00:16:30.310
true positive rate
is on the left.

00:16:30.310 --> 00:16:35.230
True positive rate means, of all
the people who come to my bank

00:16:35.230 --> 00:16:37.990
and would pay me back,
what is the percentage

00:16:37.990 --> 00:16:41.010
of people I give loans to?

00:16:41.010 --> 00:16:42.610
So I can play with this stuff.

00:16:42.610 --> 00:16:44.660
I can play with
them independently.

00:16:44.660 --> 00:16:47.950
And the other thing I want to
make sure, because I'm a bank,

00:16:47.950 --> 00:16:51.440
is I want to make a profit
at the end of the day.

00:16:51.440 --> 00:16:55.570
So how do I do these things,
and still, how can I be fair?

00:16:55.570 --> 00:16:59.200
So we put some presets
here at-- on the left.

00:16:59.200 --> 00:17:01.500
One is, if you don't
want to be fair at all,

00:17:01.500 --> 00:17:03.940
click on maximum profit.

00:17:03.940 --> 00:17:04.839
There it is.

00:17:04.839 --> 00:17:06.670
That's what your
thresholds should be,

00:17:06.670 --> 00:17:09.130
given the distributions.

00:17:09.130 --> 00:17:13.569
If you do want to be fair,
maybe what you want to do

00:17:13.569 --> 00:17:15.940
is you want to be group unaware.

00:17:15.940 --> 00:17:17.859
So I clicked on Group
Unaware, and you

00:17:17.859 --> 00:17:20.410
can see that what
this means is I

00:17:20.410 --> 00:17:23.770
have the same thresholds
for both populations.

00:17:23.770 --> 00:17:25.599
So I don't care if you're blue.

00:17:25.599 --> 00:17:27.319
I don't care if you're orange.

00:17:27.319 --> 00:17:30.130
You're going to be treated
the same because, that's

00:17:30.130 --> 00:17:35.110
the most fair thing--
that's the fair thing to do.

00:17:35.110 --> 00:17:37.570
That could be,
except that when we

00:17:37.570 --> 00:17:41.870
start looking at the
positive rates here,

00:17:41.870 --> 00:17:45.370
I can see that, of all the
people who pay me back--

00:17:45.370 --> 00:17:47.200
the blue people--

00:17:47.200 --> 00:17:51.970
I give loans to 81%
of them, versus only

00:17:51.970 --> 00:17:54.370
60% of the orange people.

00:17:57.210 --> 00:17:58.530
Is that fair?

00:17:58.530 --> 00:18:00.660
Well, maybe the next
thing you could try

00:18:00.660 --> 00:18:04.380
is something called
demographic parity.

00:18:04.380 --> 00:18:09.190
And demographic parity optimizes
for the same positive rate.

00:18:09.190 --> 00:18:11.490
So of all the blue and
all the orange people

00:18:11.490 --> 00:18:15.270
who come to my
bank, I try to give

00:18:15.270 --> 00:18:19.500
the same percentage of loans.

00:18:19.500 --> 00:18:21.690
Again, what this
means is my thresholds

00:18:21.690 --> 00:18:26.120
are different-- quite different
for these different groups.

00:18:26.120 --> 00:18:26.780
Is that fair?

00:18:26.780 --> 00:18:29.660
Maybe, maybe not-- the
last thing here I have

00:18:29.660 --> 00:18:31.910
is equal opportunity,
which optimizes

00:18:31.910 --> 00:18:33.450
for a true positive rate.

00:18:33.450 --> 00:18:37.100
So anyway, long
story short, there

00:18:37.100 --> 00:18:42.140
are many different ways
to think about being fair.

00:18:42.140 --> 00:18:45.890
And how do you translate
those values into math

00:18:45.890 --> 00:18:49.550
for deciding in a
scenario like this?

00:18:49.550 --> 00:18:53.030
The point of doing this
was that Martin and I

00:18:53.030 --> 00:18:58.490
were interested if, with a
very simple abstract scenario

00:18:58.490 --> 00:19:01.130
like this, with a simple
visualization that people

00:19:01.130 --> 00:19:02.780
could just play with--

00:19:02.780 --> 00:19:06.350
if they could start to
develop a certain intuition

00:19:06.350 --> 00:19:09.560
for the trade-offs that happen
when you're actually trying

00:19:09.560 --> 00:19:12.170
to decide this on a system.

00:19:12.170 --> 00:19:16.460
And it was interesting to see
that this simple visualization

00:19:16.460 --> 00:19:18.630
went kind of viral on the web.

00:19:18.630 --> 00:19:22.130
And we started seeing people
have these really interesting

00:19:22.130 --> 00:19:24.200
discussions of,
oh, wow, I always

00:19:24.200 --> 00:19:26.900
thought that demographic
parity was the way to go,

00:19:26.900 --> 00:19:28.400
but now I see that--

00:19:28.400 --> 00:19:32.990
whatever-- equal opportunity
is maybe a more interesting way

00:19:32.990 --> 00:19:34.560
to think about fairness.

00:19:34.560 --> 00:19:38.480
And just to be clear, there
are many, many more metrics

00:19:38.480 --> 00:19:42.680
of fairness that have
come up since then also

00:19:42.680 --> 00:19:45.830
in the literature.

00:19:45.830 --> 00:19:49.940
And I think that's
not going to stop.

00:19:49.940 --> 00:19:52.760
Another part of the conversation
that we saw online, that

00:19:52.760 --> 00:19:58.160
is, I think, very important,
is people realizing, oh,

00:19:58.160 --> 00:20:02.010
and you can't have
all of these at once.

00:20:02.010 --> 00:20:04.610
There is no perfect solution.

00:20:04.610 --> 00:20:08.840
It's always a trade-off, and
you're going to have to choose.

00:20:08.840 --> 00:20:10.700
And so we started
getting emails.

00:20:10.700 --> 00:20:12.680
And so for instance,
one of the emails

00:20:12.680 --> 00:20:18.080
we got was from a Criminal
Justice Department in the US,

00:20:18.080 --> 00:20:21.220
saying, we saw
this visualization.

00:20:21.220 --> 00:20:24.260
Do you think we could
create something

00:20:24.260 --> 00:20:29.540
like this for our own department
so that we start to understand

00:20:29.540 --> 00:20:32.180
what the trade-offs
are that we're making

00:20:32.180 --> 00:20:34.130
with the systems we're using?

00:20:34.130 --> 00:20:38.180
Obviously, we're
not a bank, but is

00:20:38.180 --> 00:20:41.330
this-- this would be
a very powerful way

00:20:41.330 --> 00:20:45.890
to have people in the department
engage with these questions.

00:20:45.890 --> 00:20:50.990
And so based on this and
on the reaction we got--

00:20:50.990 --> 00:20:51.840
let me go back--

00:20:56.200 --> 00:20:58.930
we decided to go from
that toy simulation

00:20:58.930 --> 00:21:02.320
to actually trying to put
together an actual tool that

00:21:02.320 --> 00:21:07.450
allowed you to do some of this
playing around with your model

00:21:07.450 --> 00:21:12.170
and with testing your model
for different scenarios.

00:21:12.170 --> 00:21:16.390
And so this is a tool we
created called the What-If Tool.

00:21:16.390 --> 00:21:22.750
And this is a code free,
probing tool
for machine learning systems

00:21:22.750 --> 00:21:27.430
that allows you to test for
some of these scenarios--

00:21:27.430 --> 00:21:30.010
to say things
like, oh, what if I

00:21:30.010 --> 00:21:34.780
want demographic parity in the
different slices of my data?

00:21:34.780 --> 00:21:38.660
What thresholds should
I use in my model?

00:21:38.660 --> 00:21:43.690
It allows you to do things like
click on a data point and say--

00:21:43.690 --> 00:21:46.810
for instance, let's go
back to the loan scenario--

00:21:46.810 --> 00:21:51.320
and say, oh, this person
did not get a loan.

00:21:51.320 --> 00:21:54.250
Let me go back to
the next slide.

00:21:54.250 --> 00:21:58.030
Here-- this person
did not get a loan.

00:21:58.030 --> 00:22:02.740
Who is the nearest person to
that one that did get a loan,

00:22:02.740 --> 00:22:04.910
and what is the delta there?

00:22:04.910 --> 00:22:09.970
So a little notion of
counterfactual, if you
will, without the causality--

00:22:09.970 --> 00:22:13.210
that's a separate conversation--
but just this notion

00:22:13.210 --> 00:22:16.420
of starting to try to understand
what's happening with these

00:22:16.420 --> 00:22:20.440
systems, how they are behaving,
asking a bunch of alternative

00:22:20.440 --> 00:22:24.550
scenarios, trying a
bunch of edge cases,

00:22:24.550 --> 00:22:28.100
and also playing with
notions of fairness--

00:22:28.100 --> 00:22:32.750
of how different slices
of your data set are doing

00:22:32.750 --> 00:22:36.220
vis a vis one another.

00:22:36.220 --> 00:22:41.200
This too is open-source, so
it is available for anyone.

00:22:41.200 --> 00:22:45.700
It is also being integrated
into Google's Cloud AI

00:22:45.700 --> 00:22:49.060
so that anyone
using that platform

00:22:49.060 --> 00:22:52.720
can now play with these
alternative scenarios and test

00:22:52.720 --> 00:22:56.770
things for themselves before
they decide to launch something

00:22:56.770 --> 00:22:59.803
or to try to debug situations.

00:22:59.803 --> 00:23:01.720
And I think one of the
really important things

00:23:01.720 --> 00:23:03.550
here too is the
fact that we're not

00:23:03.550 --> 00:23:08.770
expecting that you need to
be a PhD in machine learning

00:23:08.770 --> 00:23:11.890
to ask these probing questions.

00:23:11.890 --> 00:23:17.890
Many more people should be
engaging and experimenting

00:23:17.890 --> 00:23:21.950
with the way these
systems behave.

00:23:21.950 --> 00:23:23.620
MARTIN WATTENBERG:
So I think that
the story so far

00:23:23.620 --> 00:23:26.890
is, you're seeing how
important data analysis is

00:23:26.890 --> 00:23:28.120
in all of this.

00:23:28.120 --> 00:23:30.760
And then I think, also, when
you look at machine learning

00:23:30.760 --> 00:23:33.340
fairness, there's this
issue, number one,

00:23:33.340 --> 00:23:35.590
that trying to make
the world better,

00:23:35.590 --> 00:23:38.530
to try to do the right
thing involves statistics.

00:23:38.530 --> 00:23:40.922
However, also, if you
are deploying models,

00:23:40.922 --> 00:23:42.880
this is something you
now have to think about--

00:23:42.880 --> 00:23:46.300
or should have been thinking
about all along, indeed.

00:23:46.300 --> 00:23:48.160
And I think that's
an important change

00:23:48.160 --> 00:23:50.260
of mindset for many
people that we've seen.

00:23:50.260 --> 00:23:53.313
And again, statistics
is a key part of this.

00:23:53.313 --> 00:23:54.980
There's something
else going on, though,

00:23:54.980 --> 00:23:57.370
which is that,
using data analysis,

00:23:57.370 --> 00:24:01.810
you can actually end up finding
new ways to control AI systems.

00:24:01.810 --> 00:24:03.790
And to explain that,
we're going to take

00:24:03.790 --> 00:24:06.790
a journey through
high dimensional space

00:24:06.790 --> 00:24:10.220
to understand the super power.

00:24:10.220 --> 00:24:12.890
So let's talk about
data in high dimensions,

00:24:12.890 --> 00:24:16.370
and we're going to introduce a
couple of things in a row here.

00:24:16.370 --> 00:24:18.190
Let's start with a warm up.

00:24:18.190 --> 00:24:20.680
And for our warm up,
we're going to visualize

00:24:20.680 --> 00:24:23.290
that another famous
hello world data set

00:24:23.290 --> 00:24:26.260
from machine learning,
a data set of little 28

00:24:26.260 --> 00:24:30.250
by 28 icons that represent
handwritten digits that

00:24:30.250 --> 00:24:34.030
is often used to test a very
simple machine learning models.

00:24:34.030 --> 00:24:35.470
And what we're
going to talk about

00:24:35.470 --> 00:24:38.110
is how you can think
about each of these images

00:24:38.110 --> 00:24:40.430
as a vector in high
dimensional space.

00:24:40.430 --> 00:24:43.900
And the trick-- which is
familiar, I'm sure, to some,

00:24:43.900 --> 00:24:45.190
if not many of you--

00:24:45.190 --> 00:24:49.070
is that we look at the pixels
and we give them all values.

00:24:49.070 --> 00:24:52.060
In this case, we'll say
black is 0, white is 1,

00:24:52.060 --> 00:24:54.970
and intermediate values get
some floating point number.

00:24:54.970 --> 00:24:59.260
And then we just list of values
of all the pixels in a row,

00:24:59.260 --> 00:25:01.340
and that gives us a vector.

00:25:01.340 --> 00:25:02.800
So for example,
this vector would

00:25:02.800 --> 00:25:05.680
turn into something
that looks like that.

00:25:05.680 --> 00:25:11.470
Other images would look--
turn into other vectors.

00:25:11.470 --> 00:25:12.670
So why have we done this?

00:25:12.670 --> 00:25:15.330
Well, this is going
to be our example data

00:25:15.330 --> 00:25:18.020
set, as we look
at a visualization

00:25:18.020 --> 00:25:21.940
that we call the
embedding projector.

00:25:21.940 --> 00:25:23.900
Let's take a look.

00:25:23.900 --> 00:25:27.430
All right, yes, so now
you see-- rotating.

00:25:27.430 --> 00:25:31.990
You are looking at
784-dimensional space here.

00:25:31.990 --> 00:25:34.450
What you are actually
saying, of course,

00:25:34.450 --> 00:25:38.320
is a two-dimensional projection
of a three-dimensional view

00:25:38.320 --> 00:25:40.510
of this high
dimensional space that

00:25:40.510 --> 00:25:43.010
is using principal component
analysis to project

00:25:43.010 --> 00:25:44.890
these linearly.

00:25:44.890 --> 00:25:47.060
And that's definitely
an interesting thing,

00:25:47.060 --> 00:25:51.220
but it also is
kind of a big blob.

00:25:51.220 --> 00:25:53.890
As much of one can
ooh and ah over this,

00:25:53.890 --> 00:25:57.170
it's a little bit
hard to make sense of.

00:25:57.170 --> 00:26:00.480
So let's take another view.

00:26:00.480 --> 00:26:05.600
Let's look at a different
way of looking at this.

00:26:05.600 --> 00:26:08.060
OK, now we're using
a method called

00:26:08.060 --> 00:26:14.940
a UMAP that is designed to
take high dimensional space,

00:26:14.940 --> 00:26:17.510
and instead of using
a linear projection,

00:26:17.510 --> 00:26:20.750
it's using a nonlinear
projection that

00:26:20.750 --> 00:26:22.822
has many complicated
properties, which

00:26:22.822 --> 00:26:24.030
we'll talk about in a moment.

00:26:24.030 --> 00:26:26.780
But the basic idea is that
it-- one thing it does

00:26:26.780 --> 00:26:29.330
tend to preserve is clustering.

00:26:29.330 --> 00:26:34.680
And so one thing that I can do
is, since this is our data set

00:26:34.680 --> 00:26:37.910
and we have labels,
I can label--

00:26:37.910 --> 00:26:41.840
I can color these
digits by their label.

00:26:41.840 --> 00:26:46.160
And that lets us easily see,
for example, that in general,

00:26:46.160 --> 00:26:47.733
all the 1's are
at a certain area,

00:26:47.733 --> 00:26:49.650
all the 4's are in another
area, and so forth.

00:26:49.650 --> 00:26:52.670
But we can also say it doesn't
completely get it right.

00:26:52.670 --> 00:26:54.260
This 3, it clearly
thinks, belongs

00:26:54.260 --> 00:26:57.790
with the 2's, this 8 looks
like a 2, and so forth.

00:26:57.790 --> 00:27:01.340
Already I think this
view can tell you

00:27:01.340 --> 00:27:04.230
a bunch of interesting things.

00:27:04.230 --> 00:27:08.000
One thing is just that,
apparently, these digits are

00:27:08.000 --> 00:27:09.560
actually pretty well-separated.

00:27:09.560 --> 00:27:12.590
You can see why this is not the
hardest machine learning task

00:27:12.590 --> 00:27:15.440
in the world-- that there
is just a natural clustering

00:27:15.440 --> 00:27:18.290
structure that is--

00:27:18.290 --> 00:27:21.140
even a visualization method
brings it out pretty well.

00:27:21.140 --> 00:27:23.570
One other thing that's
kind of nice, though,

00:27:23.570 --> 00:27:26.220
is that, if you look even
within these clusters,

00:27:26.220 --> 00:27:28.220
you can start to see some
interesting structure.

00:27:28.220 --> 00:27:30.223
For example, in
these 1's, you'll

00:27:30.223 --> 00:27:31.640
notice that they're
kind of tilted

00:27:31.640 --> 00:27:33.470
to the left on this side.

00:27:33.470 --> 00:27:35.900
They're tilted to the
right on this side.

00:27:35.900 --> 00:27:38.330
And you really can
start to get a sense

00:27:38.330 --> 00:27:42.400
of what the geometry looks like
in this high dimensional space.

00:27:47.110 --> 00:27:47.790
Let's see.

00:27:47.790 --> 00:27:48.900
I want to go back there.

00:27:51.640 --> 00:27:55.480
What else can we do with
high dimensional space?

00:27:55.480 --> 00:27:58.120
Well, let's see.

00:27:58.120 --> 00:28:00.130
I think, if there's
time at the end,

00:28:00.130 --> 00:28:02.890
we might I talk a little
bit about what these mean,

00:28:02.890 --> 00:28:05.140
but I think I might skip this.

00:28:05.140 --> 00:28:07.240
I will say that, among
the educational materials

00:28:07.240 --> 00:28:12.130
that we've been putting out are
step-by-step interactive essays

00:28:12.130 --> 00:28:14.650
about how to interpret
these complicated nonlinear

00:28:14.650 --> 00:28:17.950
projections, because
they're amazing, but weird.

00:28:17.950 --> 00:28:20.369
And the weirdness can be your
friend, but you have to--

00:28:20.369 --> 00:28:22.452
FERNANDA VIEGAS: And
sometimes problematic, so you

00:28:22.452 --> 00:28:23.680
want to be aware of that.

00:28:23.680 --> 00:28:25.870
Yeah.

00:28:25.870 --> 00:28:30.550
MARTIN WATTENBERG:
OK, so another kind of word
in high dimensional object,

00:28:30.550 --> 00:28:32.760
which turns out to
be very, very useful,

00:28:32.760 --> 00:28:34.600
is something called
a word embedding.

00:28:34.600 --> 00:28:37.750
So word embedding-- it's
sort of weird terminology,

00:28:37.750 --> 00:28:39.940
but what it just means
is you're mapping words

00:28:39.940 --> 00:28:42.490
into points in high
dimensional space, much the way

00:28:42.490 --> 00:28:44.790
we mapped those images.

00:28:44.790 --> 00:28:47.290
However, with images, there was
a pretty straightforward way

00:28:47.290 --> 00:28:50.500
of doing it by using pixels.

00:28:50.500 --> 00:28:52.420
With words, it's actually
sort of complicated.

00:28:52.420 --> 00:28:54.580
And I won't get into
exactly how that is done.

00:28:54.580 --> 00:28:55.710
There are actually a
couple of different ways,

00:28:55.710 --> 00:28:57.085
and the interesting
thing is they

00:28:57.085 --> 00:28:59.800
often pretty similar
results, if you do it well.

00:28:59.800 --> 00:29:03.190
The idea is usually to make
sure that related words end up

00:29:03.190 --> 00:29:04.730
in near each other.

00:29:04.730 --> 00:29:07.990
And in fact, it turns
out you can do this.

00:29:07.990 --> 00:29:11.620
However, when people figured
out ways to make that happen,

00:29:11.620 --> 00:29:14.230
they discovered a really
interesting and surprising

00:29:14.230 --> 00:29:17.890
fact, which is not only can
you get a situation where

00:29:17.890 --> 00:29:19.690
similar words are
near each other,

00:29:19.690 --> 00:29:21.700
but almost as a byproduct--

00:29:21.700 --> 00:29:24.370
seemingly as an accident--

00:29:24.370 --> 00:29:27.083
you get meaningful
directions in space.

00:29:27.083 --> 00:29:28.750
And so the image that
you're seeing here

00:29:28.750 --> 00:29:32.890
is a figure from one very
famous early paper in which they

00:29:32.890 --> 00:29:36.700
showed that there's a direction
in space that corresponds

00:29:36.700 --> 00:29:39.760
to the relationship of
a capital to the country

00:29:39.760 --> 00:29:43.690
it is a capital of,
which is really amazing.

00:29:43.690 --> 00:29:45.970
OK, so let's take a look--

00:29:45.970 --> 00:29:48.220
just because we have
our visualization up--

00:29:48.220 --> 00:29:51.792
of what those word
embedding is look like.

00:29:51.792 --> 00:29:53.750
And I'm going to show
this in a couple of ways.

00:29:57.690 --> 00:30:00.990
This is just a raw PCA view.

00:30:08.330 --> 00:30:10.460
This is a UMAP view.

00:30:10.460 --> 00:30:13.340
And let's actually play
with us a little bit.

00:30:13.340 --> 00:30:15.770
Let us explore this,
because I think

00:30:15.770 --> 00:30:17.450
it really gives a sense of--

00:30:17.450 --> 00:30:19.160
you're seeing the
English language here.

00:30:19.160 --> 00:30:21.290
This is the geometry of English.

00:30:21.290 --> 00:30:22.770
And what can we say about it?

00:30:22.770 --> 00:30:24.230
Well, there's obviously clumps.

00:30:24.230 --> 00:30:26.270
Let's go spelunking
a little bit.

00:30:26.270 --> 00:30:27.270
What is this clump here?

00:30:27.270 --> 00:30:29.480
Oh, look, a bunch of names--

00:30:29.480 --> 00:30:32.450
seems like largely
maybe boys names here.

00:30:32.450 --> 00:30:34.440
And then let's see.

00:30:34.440 --> 00:30:35.540
We get some other names--

00:30:35.540 --> 00:30:36.310
FERNANDA VIEGAS: It might
be hard for people--

00:30:36.310 --> 00:30:38.060
MARTIN WATTERNBERG:
Yeah, I'll read them--

00:30:38.060 --> 00:30:40.760
Lloyd, Alex, Matt, Joel.

00:30:40.760 --> 00:30:44.120
What about this cluster here?

00:30:44.120 --> 00:30:48.490
Horns, percussion,
guitar, quartet, bass--

00:30:48.490 --> 00:30:50.840
we found the music
cluster over there.

00:30:50.840 --> 00:30:51.550
What about these?

00:30:51.550 --> 00:30:53.030
Oh, look, we have some numbers.

00:30:53.030 --> 00:30:56.090
Oops-- let's see if we
can zoom in on them.

00:30:56.090 --> 00:30:58.317
0, 1, 2, 3--

00:30:58.317 --> 00:31:00.650
we can actually see-- and in
fact, if we zoomed in more,

00:31:00.650 --> 00:31:03.410
we can almost see a little
bit of a linear structure

00:31:03.410 --> 00:31:04.700
representing those.

00:31:04.700 --> 00:31:06.830
We have some other things here.

00:31:06.830 --> 00:31:09.440
Let's see-- infinity,
angle, math---- look,

00:31:09.440 --> 00:31:12.870
we found the tech world here.

00:31:12.870 --> 00:31:14.470
So there really is everything.

00:31:14.470 --> 00:31:18.530
And this gives you a sense
of just how much information

00:31:18.530 --> 00:31:20.540
is encoded here.

00:31:20.540 --> 00:31:28.340
But now I want to show one
more thing, and that is--

00:31:28.340 --> 00:31:33.020
let's do something tricky.

00:31:33.020 --> 00:31:35.720
Let's do a custom embedding.

00:31:35.720 --> 00:31:38.870
And the way this
works is we actually

00:31:38.870 --> 00:31:44.960
can define an axis in terms
of one word to another word.

00:31:44.960 --> 00:31:47.790
Right now, it's just a 2D
projection, it's linear,

00:31:47.790 --> 00:31:50.780
and both axes are random.

00:31:50.780 --> 00:31:53.870
But let's actually search
for a word and look

00:31:53.870 --> 00:31:55.410
at some neighbors.

00:31:55.410 --> 00:31:56.970
Let's see, what's a good one?

00:31:56.970 --> 00:31:58.080
How about science?

00:32:01.907 --> 00:32:03.550
I'll click on this.

00:32:03.550 --> 00:32:06.200
We'll isolate those 100 points.

00:32:06.200 --> 00:32:09.160
And now, we can see the
neighbors of the word science

00:32:09.160 --> 00:32:10.900
in PCA.

00:32:10.900 --> 00:32:13.570
But now let's actually
do a custom thing.

00:32:13.570 --> 00:32:18.290
And we can play this little
game where, say, we look at--

00:32:18.290 --> 00:32:24.270
let's look at man on the
left and woman on the right.

00:32:24.270 --> 00:32:28.080
And suddenly, we see
something really interesting--

00:32:28.080 --> 00:32:31.740
that now, the left right of
the x-axis, left to right,

00:32:31.740 --> 00:32:33.900
has things that
it's thinking closer

00:32:33.900 --> 00:32:36.750
to the word man versus woman.

00:32:36.750 --> 00:32:38.730
And this is actually
a little bit sad

00:32:38.730 --> 00:32:42.750
that you can technical is
it's most male word here

00:32:42.750 --> 00:32:44.730
in this neighborhood.

00:32:44.730 --> 00:32:47.880
And meanwhile, on
the female side,

00:32:47.880 --> 00:32:51.980
although we do a scientist, we
see religion, author, story,

00:32:51.980 --> 00:32:53.190
psychology.

00:32:53.190 --> 00:32:54.960
And this is actually
a sobering moment.

00:32:54.960 --> 00:32:57.613
When people started to realize
this about these embeddings,

00:32:57.613 --> 00:33:00.030
I think they just realized how
serious things had become--

00:33:00.030 --> 00:33:03.630
that these things had been
trained on huge corpora

00:33:03.630 --> 00:33:07.110
of existing words,
language, and those had

00:33:07.110 --> 00:33:09.570
contained a bunch of biases.

00:33:09.570 --> 00:33:13.980
And this new word embedding
was very efficiently

00:33:13.980 --> 00:33:16.870
encoding some of those.

00:33:16.870 --> 00:33:21.120
So in fact, we can show a
couple of other examples.

00:33:21.120 --> 00:33:23.340
This is the neighborhood
of engineers--

00:33:23.340 --> 00:33:26.390
shows many of the
similar things.

00:33:26.390 --> 00:33:28.240
If you do a neighborhood
pf math..

00:33:28.240 --> 00:33:29.430
FERNANDA VIEGAS: You might
want to read it out loud.

00:33:29.430 --> 00:33:30.472
MARTIN WATTERNBERG: Yeah.

00:33:30.472 --> 00:33:33.210
You can see geometry,
computational on the man side--

00:33:33.210 --> 00:33:38.190
again, psychology, art,
library on the woman side.

00:33:38.190 --> 00:33:44.220
And it's not even just the
big famous biases, like gender

00:33:44.220 --> 00:33:46.140
or something, but you can--

00:33:46.140 --> 00:33:47.975
even something
like old and new--

00:33:47.975 --> 00:33:50.100
and not with people, you
can see interesting things

00:33:50.100 --> 00:33:52.950
that are meaningful, like
with-- if I put in a book

00:33:52.950 --> 00:33:55.620
and look at its neighbors,
I see-- for old,

00:33:55.620 --> 00:33:58.950
I see things like
poem and manuscript.

00:33:58.950 --> 00:34:03.270
On the new side, I see
company, for instance-- magic.

00:34:03.270 --> 00:34:05.790
And you can start to
see it's picking up

00:34:05.790 --> 00:34:08.429
on important associations,
but in a way that

00:34:08.429 --> 00:34:10.080
is potentially problematic.

00:34:13.800 --> 00:34:15.929
So we have this
thing that is powerful.

00:34:15.929 --> 00:34:17.813
It requires a lot of analysis.

00:34:17.813 --> 00:34:19.230
But let's think
about how we could

00:34:19.230 --> 00:34:22.600
use this idea of meaningful
directions in other ways,

00:34:22.600 --> 00:34:24.750
now that we have it.

00:34:24.750 --> 00:34:27.480
So one way, it turns
out, is to interpret

00:34:27.480 --> 00:34:29.190
what's going on inside
of neural networks

00:34:29.190 --> 00:34:32.010
so that they're not,
in fact, black boxes.

00:34:32.010 --> 00:34:35.310
And I'd like to very quickly
fly through a method called

00:34:35.310 --> 00:34:40.560
TCAV, published in ICML 2018.

00:34:40.560 --> 00:34:42.330
And the idea behind
this method is

00:34:42.330 --> 00:34:45.600
to take this insight that, OK,
if things like word embeddings

00:34:45.600 --> 00:34:50.010
have directions that
correspond to things that

00:34:50.010 --> 00:34:54.120
are meaningful to humans, like
capital cities to capitals,

00:34:54.120 --> 00:34:56.429
maybe there are other
types of embeddings

00:34:56.429 --> 00:34:58.140
that have that same
property, and we

00:34:58.140 --> 00:35:00.810
can use that to our advantage.

00:35:00.810 --> 00:35:05.190
So I'll illustrate this by
imagining a question you might

00:35:05.190 --> 00:35:06.930
ask of an image classifier.

00:35:06.930 --> 00:35:09.480
Let's say it looks at
a picture of a zebra,

00:35:09.480 --> 00:35:13.260
and you wonder, why did it
think that thing was a zebra?

00:35:13.260 --> 00:35:15.630
So there's sort of a bad
answer to this question

00:35:15.630 --> 00:35:16.770
and a good answer.

00:35:16.770 --> 00:35:19.080
The bad answer is if it
told you something like,

00:35:19.080 --> 00:35:23.040
it with this particular pixel
or these sets of pixels,

00:35:23.040 --> 00:35:23.970
because that's not--

00:35:23.970 --> 00:35:26.580
those are not terms that
a human could understand.

00:35:26.580 --> 00:35:29.760
A much better answer would be if
it could say something like, it

00:35:29.760 --> 00:35:33.060
was striped, and that helped.

00:35:33.060 --> 00:35:35.130
Of course, it's very
hard to figure out

00:35:35.130 --> 00:35:37.780
how to extract that answer.

00:35:37.780 --> 00:35:39.630
However, there is
a way to do it.

00:35:39.630 --> 00:35:42.000
So let's follow
what happens, if you

00:35:42.000 --> 00:35:43.860
have a neural network
that is recognizing

00:35:43.860 --> 00:35:45.930
an image, like zebra.

00:35:45.930 --> 00:35:47.890
So you put in an image--

00:35:47.890 --> 00:35:49.830
let's say it's an
image of a zebra--

00:35:49.830 --> 00:35:51.337
and then it will--

00:35:51.337 --> 00:35:53.670
you put it into a neural
network, and what is shown here

00:35:53.670 --> 00:35:56.580
is a little cartoon of
one famous network called

00:35:56.580 --> 00:35:58.100
Inception.

00:35:58.100 --> 00:36:02.310
And the idea is that, in this
kind of network, you have--

00:36:02.310 --> 00:36:06.380
the image get transformed
layer by layer.

00:36:06.380 --> 00:36:09.270
You think of each layer as
performing a transformation

00:36:09.270 --> 00:36:10.440
of Euclidean space.

00:36:13.390 --> 00:36:15.390
For if you put something
like zebra through it--

00:36:15.390 --> 00:36:16.650
showed an image--

00:36:16.650 --> 00:36:18.810
in each of these
layers, you can think

00:36:18.810 --> 00:36:21.750
of the state of the layers
as a set of activations

00:36:21.750 --> 00:36:23.640
of the neurons, and
those activations

00:36:23.640 --> 00:36:25.778
form a vector in high
dimensional space.

00:36:25.778 --> 00:36:28.320
So you can see how this starts
to relate to what we were just

00:36:28.320 --> 00:36:29.767
looking at.

00:36:29.767 --> 00:36:31.350
And then what's
interesting is, if you

00:36:31.350 --> 00:36:33.390
put a whole bunch
of images through,

00:36:33.390 --> 00:36:36.660
you get a bunch of points in
this high dimensional space.

00:36:36.660 --> 00:36:38.670
And you can start
playing this game

00:36:38.670 --> 00:36:42.040
with meaningful directions.

00:36:42.040 --> 00:36:44.350
So let's show how this works.

00:36:44.350 --> 00:36:46.500
So the idea is that,
let's say there's

00:36:46.500 --> 00:36:49.470
a concept you're interested
in-- for example, stripes

00:36:49.470 --> 00:36:51.995
or the presence of stripes.

00:36:51.995 --> 00:36:53.495
You don't even have
to try to define

00:36:53.495 --> 00:36:56.640
to the computer in
language what stripes is.

00:36:56.640 --> 00:37:01.110
You could just show it a bunch
of examples of striped images.

00:37:01.110 --> 00:37:03.390
And then you put
those striped images

00:37:03.390 --> 00:37:05.850
through your neural
network, and then--

00:37:05.850 --> 00:37:07.470
FERNANDA VIEGAS:
And one important thing--

00:37:07.470 --> 00:37:09.390
these are not images of zebras.

00:37:09.390 --> 00:37:11.580
This is just any
stripes anywhere.

00:37:11.580 --> 00:37:13.860
MARTIN WATTENBERG:
We've done this literally
with striped ties,

00:37:13.860 --> 00:37:16.830
and striped shirts,
and abstract stripes.

00:37:16.830 --> 00:37:18.130
That's a good point.

00:37:18.130 --> 00:37:20.150
And then what you'll
get is a cluster

00:37:20.150 --> 00:37:22.760
the images in this
space of activations

00:37:22.760 --> 00:37:25.990
in an intermediate layer
that represents stripes.

00:37:25.990 --> 00:37:28.470
And then you can also put a
bunch of random other images

00:37:28.470 --> 00:37:31.530
through, and train a
classifier to distinguish

00:37:31.530 --> 00:37:33.810
between the stripes
and the non-stripes.

00:37:33.810 --> 00:37:36.360
And that gives you what's
called a concept activation

00:37:36.360 --> 00:37:40.380
vector, a direction in space
that represents a concept.

00:37:40.380 --> 00:37:42.030
And then, this is
what's cool, is

00:37:42.030 --> 00:37:44.280
that you can start
calculating with that.

00:37:44.280 --> 00:37:46.920
That vector is something--
you can, for example, take

00:37:46.920 --> 00:37:49.590
directional derivative to
understand the sensitivity

00:37:49.590 --> 00:37:51.330
to this concept.

00:37:51.330 --> 00:37:54.450
So computers like it,
but humans like it too,

00:37:54.450 --> 00:37:57.270
because it's a human-friendly
concept of stripes

00:37:57.270 --> 00:38:00.360
that they're getting
an answer in terms of.

00:38:00.360 --> 00:38:03.090
So this turns out to actually
be a very useful technique

00:38:03.090 --> 00:38:03.600
in general.

00:38:03.600 --> 00:38:06.593
There's a lot more we
can say, but what's

00:38:06.593 --> 00:38:08.010
really interesting
is that you can

00:38:08.010 --> 00:38:11.640
go from just interpreting
or understanding directly

00:38:11.640 --> 00:38:13.423
to control.

00:38:13.423 --> 00:38:14.340
FERNANDA VIEGAS: Yeah.

00:38:14.340 --> 00:38:18.540
So imagine that you start with
this super high dimensional

00:38:18.540 --> 00:38:22.590
space, and you find
meaning in that space--

00:38:22.590 --> 00:38:25.260
meaning that is also
meaningful to humans.

00:38:25.260 --> 00:38:27.720
So what I want to
show you next is

00:38:27.720 --> 00:38:33.210
an example of a project where
we used that for health care.

00:38:33.210 --> 00:38:35.370
So here's the scenario.

00:38:35.370 --> 00:38:37.680
We were working with
pathologists trying

00:38:37.680 --> 00:38:41.310
to diagnose cancer,
and the way they work

00:38:41.310 --> 00:38:44.820
is they will look at
a slide of a patient--

00:38:44.820 --> 00:38:48.070
very much like the large
image you see here--

00:38:48.070 --> 00:38:50.760
and then they will try--
they will very, very closely

00:38:50.760 --> 00:38:53.940
analyze this image and
try to understand, one,

00:38:53.940 --> 00:38:56.250
do I believe there--
is there a cancer here?

00:38:56.250 --> 00:38:59.520
And if so, what grade of cancer?

00:38:59.520 --> 00:39:03.000
This is what's called Gleason
1, Gleason 2, Gleason 4.

00:39:05.610 --> 00:39:09.030
And so even though there has--
there is an extensive set

00:39:09.030 --> 00:39:14.700
of work that has been done with
optimizing algorithmic trends--

00:39:14.700 --> 00:39:18.540
performance to try to look
at an image like this

00:39:18.540 --> 00:39:20.520
and say, yes, there
is cancer, no, there

00:39:20.520 --> 00:39:23.160
is no cancer, much
less attention

00:39:23.160 --> 00:39:28.230
had been paid to actually, what
kind of information and what

00:39:28.230 --> 00:39:30.300
kind of interactions
would doctors

00:39:30.300 --> 00:39:36.240
like to get out from a
dialogue with these systems?

00:39:36.240 --> 00:39:39.690
Instead of just being given
a number and a probability,

00:39:39.690 --> 00:39:43.170
do they actually want
to interact and direct

00:39:43.170 --> 00:39:44.490
these systems?

00:39:44.490 --> 00:39:47.740
It turns out that, yes,
they would like to do this.

00:39:47.740 --> 00:39:51.570
And part of the reason why
they want to do stuff like this

00:39:51.570 --> 00:39:55.680
is because, many times,
for things like--

00:39:55.680 --> 00:39:58.110
sometimes there are
defects in these images,

00:39:58.110 --> 00:39:59.680
and they want to
say, dismiss that.

00:39:59.680 --> 00:40:01.680
I don't want you to pay
attention to that thing.

00:40:01.680 --> 00:40:03.450
That's not important.

00:40:03.450 --> 00:40:09.270
Or there are anatomical features
that they care deeply about--

00:40:09.270 --> 00:40:12.150
things like the
density of glands,

00:40:12.150 --> 00:40:14.988
or whatever other concept.

00:40:14.988 --> 00:40:15.780
And think about it.

00:40:15.780 --> 00:40:17.610
Those are high level concepts.

00:40:17.610 --> 00:40:19.920
How do you tell the machine
what I really care about

00:40:19.920 --> 00:40:23.370
or how dense the
glands are here?

00:40:23.370 --> 00:40:26.410
So you can you can do
things that have been done.

00:40:26.410 --> 00:40:28.590
We're going to talk about this.

00:40:28.590 --> 00:40:31.440
But basically, what
we did is we created

00:40:31.440 --> 00:40:35.280
a set of tools that
allowed pathologists

00:40:35.280 --> 00:40:39.030
to very quickly navigate
these highly massive data

00:40:39.030 --> 00:40:40.950
sets, because
again, what they're

00:40:40.950 --> 00:40:42.810
trying to do-- they're
looking at that slide

00:40:42.810 --> 00:40:45.630
and they are trying
to get the system

00:40:45.630 --> 00:40:49.590
to bring to them the
nearest neighbors.

00:40:49.590 --> 00:40:52.280
What is the patient
I've seen before that

00:40:52.280 --> 00:40:54.780
has been diagnosed--

00:40:54.780 --> 00:40:59.400
what is the closest case to
this case I'm seeing now?

00:40:59.400 --> 00:41:00.738
But that's not a one-shot thing.

00:41:00.738 --> 00:41:02.280
That is the thing
that's interesting.

00:41:02.280 --> 00:41:04.200
It's not just like,
automate that.

00:41:04.200 --> 00:41:08.140
So one of the things is just
allowing people to cut little--

00:41:08.140 --> 00:41:09.720
to just say, focus here.

00:41:09.720 --> 00:41:11.640
That's what I want to do.

00:41:11.640 --> 00:41:14.490
Things like these
have been done before.

00:41:14.490 --> 00:41:18.180
Or once you bring
me a bunch of cases

00:41:18.180 --> 00:41:21.510
that are like the case I
just highlighted for you,

00:41:21.510 --> 00:41:26.370
now let me go deeper into one
of those cases and bring me more

00:41:26.370 --> 00:41:29.580
closest neighbors
to those cases.

00:41:29.580 --> 00:41:33.823
So in other words, let
me navigate by example.

00:41:33.823 --> 00:41:35.490
I want you to pay
attention to this now.

00:41:35.490 --> 00:41:36.330
Now, let me go back.

00:41:36.330 --> 00:41:37.830
I want you to pay
attention to-- oh,

00:41:37.830 --> 00:41:41.940
this is interesting--
let's go that way.

00:41:41.940 --> 00:41:45.600
All of that kind of
had been done before.

00:41:45.600 --> 00:41:48.660
The thing that's
new that we can do

00:41:48.660 --> 00:41:53.730
is now, with things like CAVs,
that you just heard about,

00:41:53.730 --> 00:41:58.470
we can actually create much
more sophisticated ways

00:41:58.470 --> 00:42:02.150
of navigating these systems
in medical concepts.

00:42:02.150 --> 00:42:04.590
So working with
the pathologists,

00:42:04.590 --> 00:42:09.060
they told us that a medical
concept they deeply care about

00:42:09.060 --> 00:42:10.920
is something called stroma.

00:42:10.920 --> 00:42:11.910
I'm not a doctor.

00:42:11.910 --> 00:42:14.570
I had no idea what this
was, but for them, it

00:42:14.570 --> 00:42:15.960
was super interesting.

00:42:15.960 --> 00:42:18.150
So we created a
slider where they

00:42:18.150 --> 00:42:22.490
could say, show me cases like
this, but with more stroma,

00:42:22.490 --> 00:42:24.380
or with less stroma--

00:42:24.380 --> 00:42:26.150
or fused glands.

00:42:26.150 --> 00:42:29.450
That's another medical concept
they care deeply about.

00:42:29.450 --> 00:42:35.450
And so because, again,
these ML systems--

00:42:35.450 --> 00:42:38.510
inside these highly
massive spaces,

00:42:38.510 --> 00:42:40.730
there are meaningful
directions, we

00:42:40.730 --> 00:42:43.910
were able to map
these medical concepts

00:42:43.910 --> 00:42:49.580
to these meaningful
directions in the system,

00:42:49.580 --> 00:42:53.360
and that gave doctors a
much more powerful way

00:42:53.360 --> 00:42:55.600
of interacting with the systems.

00:42:55.600 --> 00:42:58.010
And one of the really
important things

00:42:58.010 --> 00:43:00.140
here is to actually--
for the doctor,

00:43:00.140 --> 00:43:02.270
to calibrate their trust.

00:43:02.270 --> 00:43:03.710
Should I trust this?

00:43:03.710 --> 00:43:05.240
Should I not trust this?

00:43:05.240 --> 00:43:09.230
Or even to learn how the system
works-- how does it behave?

00:43:09.230 --> 00:43:11.940
How does it think
about edge cases?

00:43:11.940 --> 00:43:14.610
And so we actually
had a user study--

00:43:14.610 --> 00:43:18.290
in this case, was
with 12 pathologists--

00:43:18.290 --> 00:43:21.710
looking both at a traditional
machine learning system

00:43:21.710 --> 00:43:25.970
that just gives you a prediction
with a confidence level,

00:43:25.970 --> 00:43:28.550
or at this system
called SMILY, where

00:43:28.550 --> 00:43:30.170
you have all these
different tools

00:43:30.170 --> 00:43:32.720
that you can interact with.

00:43:32.720 --> 00:43:35.080
And the results
were very positive.

00:43:35.080 --> 00:43:43.210
Basically, they-- all 12
pathologists preferred SMILY.

00:43:43.210 --> 00:43:50.180
The level of trust was
significantly higher.

00:43:50.180 --> 00:43:54.080
And even though you would think,
if you could only automate

00:43:54.080 --> 00:43:58.160
my work for me, I'd be done,
they actually found a lot

00:43:58.160 --> 00:44:04.640
of use in going back and
forth-- but not only that--

00:44:04.640 --> 00:44:08.360
steering the system in
ways that they cared about.

00:44:14.720 --> 00:44:17.250
MARTIN WATTERNBERG: Yeah, I
think what you see from this

00:44:17.250 --> 00:44:20.310
is the ability of
statistical analysis

00:44:20.310 --> 00:44:22.180
to actually help
steer these systems.

00:44:22.180 --> 00:44:24.570
And I want to talk to
another aspect of this.

00:44:24.570 --> 00:44:27.330
And so here, I'm going
to ask you to actually do

00:44:27.330 --> 00:44:29.670
some imagining here,
and even answer

00:44:29.670 --> 00:44:30.990
some questions in your head.

00:44:30.990 --> 00:44:33.510
So the situation
is, imagine you're

00:44:33.510 --> 00:44:36.540
a company or
nonprofit-- whatever.

00:44:36.540 --> 00:44:38.940
You're designing a
system to recommend books

00:44:38.940 --> 00:44:40.800
to library patrons.

00:44:40.800 --> 00:44:45.270
And let's imagine you have
a team of people doing this.

00:44:45.270 --> 00:44:48.420
And you have engineers.

00:44:48.420 --> 00:44:51.060
Maybe have someone who is
responsible for the design.

00:44:51.060 --> 00:44:53.370
And I mean that broadly, in
terms of user experience,

00:44:53.370 --> 00:44:55.680
or UX, as people say.

00:44:55.680 --> 00:44:58.080
And you're going over a
bunch of the big decisions

00:44:58.080 --> 00:45:00.270
and small decisions that
you'll have to make,

00:45:00.270 --> 00:45:03.990
and which ones do you need to
consult that user experience

00:45:03.990 --> 00:45:05.460
designer on?

00:45:05.460 --> 00:45:08.910
And so let's just go through
some questions, and I will--

00:45:08.910 --> 00:45:11.310
you can think about this.

00:45:11.310 --> 00:45:14.250
Does the system show one
recommendation or many?

00:45:14.250 --> 00:45:15.990
What hardware does it run on?

00:45:15.990 --> 00:45:18.150
Just answer these in your head.

00:45:18.150 --> 00:45:21.510
Do users see an explanation
for recommendations?

00:45:21.510 --> 00:45:24.320
Which database to
store your metadata in?

00:45:24.320 --> 00:45:26.430
When should you
bother that designer?

00:45:26.430 --> 00:45:29.070
What typeface do we use
for recommendations?

00:45:29.070 --> 00:45:31.050
Do we use this particular
loss function--

00:45:31.050 --> 00:45:33.720
which I will not read out loud?

00:45:33.720 --> 00:45:35.400
It's tech.

00:45:35.400 --> 00:45:38.160
Do we allow users to
like a recommendation,

00:45:38.160 --> 00:45:42.960
and should our L2
regularization constant be 9.83?

00:45:42.960 --> 00:45:45.810
So you might have
some answers in mind.

00:45:45.810 --> 00:45:48.682
Some, I think, are
actually kind of obvious.

00:45:48.682 --> 00:45:50.640
Clearly, you should talk
to your designer about

00:45:50.640 --> 00:45:52.710
whether you show one
recommendation or many.

00:45:52.710 --> 00:45:55.380
They should be involved in that.

00:45:55.380 --> 00:45:58.380
I think some are not
really design issues,

00:45:58.380 --> 00:46:01.880
like what database you
use to store metadata.

00:46:01.880 --> 00:46:03.140
Their time is precious.

00:46:03.140 --> 00:46:05.290
They don't need to
weigh in on that.

00:46:05.290 --> 00:46:08.340
However, there is one
weird question here,

00:46:08.340 --> 00:46:10.570
which is the loss function.

00:46:10.570 --> 00:46:13.330
And we would argue
that, in many cases,

00:46:13.330 --> 00:46:19.400
this may be the most important
UX decision of all, which

00:46:19.400 --> 00:46:21.320
seems very counterintuitive.

00:46:21.320 --> 00:46:25.760
Why would a chunk of math
be a user experience issue?

00:46:25.760 --> 00:46:28.790
And the answer is
that, essentially,

00:46:28.790 --> 00:46:31.190
in a machine learning
system, your loss function

00:46:31.190 --> 00:46:36.470
expresses the goal of
what your system is doing.

00:46:36.470 --> 00:46:39.110
And that goal
exactly what

00:46:39.110 --> 00:46:40.778
happens in practice,
that tells you

00:46:40.778 --> 00:46:42.320
whether people are
more likely to see

00:46:42.320 --> 00:46:44.870
bad recommendations
or good ones,

00:46:44.870 --> 00:46:47.240
are they going to see
similar ones or diverse--

00:46:47.240 --> 00:46:50.540
or more diverse ones, and
many, many other aspects.

00:46:50.540 --> 00:46:52.970
We see this anywhere
recommendation systems

00:46:52.970 --> 00:46:54.410
are used.

00:46:54.410 --> 00:46:58.130
And that's, again, kind
of a sobering thought,

00:46:58.130 --> 00:47:00.640
because how do you
understand this thing?

00:47:00.640 --> 00:47:02.190
Can a mathematician
just stare at it

00:47:02.190 --> 00:47:03.410
and figure out the effects?

00:47:03.410 --> 00:47:04.760
No.

00:47:04.760 --> 00:47:08.510
But a statistician,
you've got a good chance.

00:47:08.510 --> 00:47:12.810
If you look at pilot tests,
user practice, run simulations,

00:47:12.810 --> 00:47:15.720
you've got a whole bunch
of tools to look at.

00:47:15.720 --> 00:47:18.140
And so what this says, I
think, in the new world

00:47:18.140 --> 00:47:20.390
that we find
ourselves in with AI,

00:47:20.390 --> 00:47:22.700
is that statisticians have an--

00:47:22.700 --> 00:47:25.670
anyone who works with data has
an incredibly important role

00:47:25.670 --> 00:47:27.530
to play.

00:47:27.530 --> 00:47:31.040
The models that you create
are having broad effects

00:47:31.040 --> 00:47:32.150
on all sorts of people.

00:47:32.150 --> 00:47:34.460
As you think about
what stakeholders,

00:47:34.460 --> 00:47:36.920
who you want to take care
of, think about also,

00:47:36.920 --> 00:47:37.950
who you want to talk to.

00:47:37.950 --> 00:47:41.270
Like, as you're writing a
formula for your loss function,

00:47:41.270 --> 00:47:43.580
maybe you should talk
to a UX designer.

00:47:43.580 --> 00:47:45.920
As you do that,
get their opinion.

00:47:45.920 --> 00:47:48.380
That could be as good a
moment for a user test

00:47:48.380 --> 00:47:50.510
or ethnography as
any other moment

00:47:50.510 --> 00:47:52.330
in the design of the system.

00:47:52.330 --> 00:47:53.390
You have a lot of power.

00:47:53.390 --> 00:47:58.190
FERNANDA VIEGAS:
[LAUGHS] And so even
though traditionally,

00:47:58.190 --> 00:48:01.280
design may have looked
like this, right,

00:48:01.280 --> 00:48:08.210
with the colors and the values,
we believe that in the current

00:48:08.210 --> 00:48:12.890
era of machine learning,
design is both of these things.

00:48:12.890 --> 00:48:13.970
It needs to be.

00:48:13.970 --> 00:48:18.150
We need to start thinking
that way, we believe.

00:48:18.150 --> 00:48:23.360
And so basically, our thought
to leave you with today

00:48:23.360 --> 00:48:28.010
is that if you work an R or
with data or with ML code,

00:48:28.010 --> 00:48:30.920
you are part of the
user experience team,

00:48:30.920 --> 00:48:32.900
whether or not you know it.

00:48:32.900 --> 00:48:36.380
And you have a lot of
power, as Martin was saying.

00:48:36.380 --> 00:48:37.470
So think about that.

00:48:37.470 --> 00:48:41.540
Think about how
you're going to bridge

00:48:41.540 --> 00:48:46.080
between the world of data and
what you see in the models,

00:48:46.080 --> 00:48:51.170
in the data logs you see, in
the data analysis you make,

00:48:51.170 --> 00:48:54.620
and what the end user
experience is going to be,

00:48:54.620 --> 00:48:59.460
because it's going to depend
more and more on this bridge.

00:48:59.460 --> 00:49:03.110
And so just to leave
you with one resource,

00:49:03.110 --> 00:49:07.400
hopefully that could be helpful.

00:49:07.400 --> 00:49:13.090
This guidebook is something
PAIR launched last May.

00:49:13.090 --> 00:49:17.870
And it's a set of
guidelines that

00:49:17.870 --> 00:49:23.120
tries to be very concrete about
what does it mean to build

00:49:23.120 --> 00:49:26.360
and ship AI -enabled products?

00:49:26.360 --> 00:49:30.290
This is something that
took over 150 googlers

00:49:30.290 --> 00:49:34.260
from all over the
company come together.

00:49:34.260 --> 00:49:39.020
We also had academics
that we consulted with.

00:49:39.020 --> 00:49:42.540
This came from a lot of
our internal experience

00:49:42.540 --> 00:49:44.330
shipping AI-enabled products.

00:49:46.880 --> 00:49:51.830
But this is something that
needs to be much, much bigger

00:49:51.830 --> 00:49:52.940
than Google.

00:49:52.940 --> 00:49:56.000
This is just the
beginning of trying

00:49:56.000 --> 00:49:58.340
to go from those
very lofty goals,

00:49:58.340 --> 00:50:01.140
like we need to make
machine learning fair.

00:50:01.140 --> 00:50:05.990
We need to make machine
learning explainable to the, OK,

00:50:05.990 --> 00:50:07.650
how do we get there?

00:50:07.650 --> 00:50:11.030
How do we do this when
I have to ship a product

00:50:11.030 --> 00:50:13.440
in the next three weeks?

00:50:13.440 --> 00:50:18.170
So this is a quick view
of the different chapters

00:50:18.170 --> 00:50:20.480
that the guidebook has.

00:50:20.480 --> 00:50:27.470
It's full of samples do's and
don'ts on the user experience

00:50:27.470 --> 00:50:31.160
side, step-by-step guidelines,
and also interactive

00:50:31.160 --> 00:50:32.600
worksheets.

00:50:32.600 --> 00:50:34.640
And the whole point
is that this is

00:50:34.640 --> 00:50:37.640
going to take an entire
team to figure out.

00:50:37.640 --> 00:50:41.540
This is not just the UXers
or the engineers or the data

00:50:41.540 --> 00:50:43.070
scientists.

00:50:43.070 --> 00:50:46.040
We're going to have to be
talking all of us together.

00:50:46.040 --> 00:50:51.370
And you all hold a lot of
power in that conversation.

00:50:51.370 --> 00:50:52.690
So that's it.

00:50:52.690 --> 00:50:53.570
Thank you so much.

00:50:53.570 --> 00:50:57.007
[APPLAUSE]

00:51:06.827 --> 00:51:10.420
HADLEY WICKHAM:
So I'm going to add a little
editorial voice to this.

00:51:10.420 --> 00:51:11.166
[LAUGHS]

00:51:11.166 --> 00:51:15.680
Anonymous asks, why are
you, as visualization experts,

00:51:15.680 --> 00:51:16.715
using pie charts?

00:51:16.715 --> 00:51:18.740
FERNANDA VIEGAS: [LAUGHS]

00:51:18.740 --> 00:51:22.440
MARTIN WATTERNBERG: Don't
get me started on pie charts.

00:51:22.440 --> 00:51:26.017
I think they are so underrated.

00:51:26.017 --> 00:51:28.100
I actually think there are
many, many good aspects

00:51:28.100 --> 00:51:29.810
of pie charts.

00:51:29.810 --> 00:51:34.550
They, in cases where you have
only a few items, like three

00:51:34.550 --> 00:51:38.360
or four, and where you're
interested in the sizes of sums

00:51:38.360 --> 00:51:40.670
of wedges that are
next to each other,

00:51:40.670 --> 00:51:43.780
I actually think they have many
advantages over bar charts.

00:51:43.780 --> 00:51:45.410
There are certain
queries you can make.

00:51:45.410 --> 00:51:48.050
Like, is there anything
that has a majority?

00:51:48.050 --> 00:51:51.110
Or are there two that have them
slices of a majority compared

00:51:51.110 --> 00:51:53.830
to a third or two others?

00:51:53.830 --> 00:51:55.550
They're much easier.

00:51:55.550 --> 00:51:58.400
If you look at the history
of research into pie charts,

00:51:58.400 --> 00:52:02.090
people have been trying,
literally since the 1920s,

00:52:02.090 --> 00:52:03.410
to prove that they're bad.

00:52:03.410 --> 00:52:04.915
And they never really have.

00:52:04.915 --> 00:52:08.134
[LAUGHS] Don't get me started.

00:52:08.134 --> 00:52:09.082
Oops.

00:52:09.082 --> 00:52:10.030
[LAUGHS]

00:52:10.030 --> 00:52:13.822
[LAUGHS]

00:52:13.822 --> 00:52:16.980
HADLEY WICKHAM:
So we have quite a few
questions just about, like,

00:52:16.980 --> 00:52:20.560
what tools did you use to
make these visualizations?

00:52:20.560 --> 00:52:27.000
FERNANDA VIEGAS:
Oh, so most of the things you
saw today are JavaScript tools.

00:52:27.990 --> 00:52:31.485
And we tend to code
things from scratch.

00:52:36.220 --> 00:52:38.520
MARTIN WATTERNBERG: I
mean, D3 is very helpful.

00:52:38.520 --> 00:52:42.090
I also think that WebGL
has been sort of amazing.

00:52:42.090 --> 00:52:45.320
Like, that's been a
huge technical advance,

00:52:45.320 --> 00:52:47.070
like that when I
think about what

00:52:47.070 --> 00:52:51.000
has changed since when I first
started doing visualization,

00:52:51.000 --> 00:52:54.540
there are things you can do,
just the number of images

00:52:54.540 --> 00:52:56.700
you can animate at
one time smoothly.

00:52:56.700 --> 00:52:58.345
That's been a big deal.

00:52:58.345 --> 00:53:00.720
FERNANDA VIEGAS: One thing
that I think we forgot to say.

00:53:00.720 --> 00:53:04.260
The embedding projector that
you saw Martin demo to date,

00:53:04.260 --> 00:53:05.590
that's also open source.

00:53:05.590 --> 00:53:06.960
That's also available.

00:53:06.960 --> 00:53:09.150
And anyone can,
yeah, play with it.

00:53:09.150 --> 00:53:10.710
HADLEY WICKHAM:
So I had a personal
question, which

00:53:10.710 --> 00:53:12.210
is it looked like
one of those parts

00:53:12.210 --> 00:53:14.470
is a ggplot2 plot.

00:53:14.470 --> 00:53:15.870
Is that correct?

00:53:15.870 --> 00:53:18.660
MARTIN WATTENBERG:
It probably is, actually, yes.

00:53:18.660 --> 00:53:23.215
[LAUGHS]

00:53:23.215 --> 00:53:25.980
HADLEY WICKHAM:
Taylor, asks, Facets
is a beautiful tool

00:53:25.980 --> 00:53:27.690
for exploring image data.

00:53:27.690 --> 00:53:29.550
What strategies do
you use when you've

00:53:29.550 --> 00:53:32.490
got nonimage data, like
400 variables trying

00:53:32.490 --> 00:53:34.390
to protect a class?

00:53:34.390 --> 00:53:39.450
MARTIN WATTENBERG:
[SIGHS] So it depends
on the type of variable.

00:53:39.450 --> 00:53:43.218
So we certainly do
a bunch of things.

00:53:43.218 --> 00:53:45.510
And that idea of, like,
slicing and dicing, you usually

00:53:45.510 --> 00:53:49.032
have to pick a couple of
variables to focus on.

00:53:49.032 --> 00:53:50.240
There are a couple of things.

00:53:50.240 --> 00:53:53.370
So one is, in many
cases, you can actually

00:53:53.370 --> 00:53:55.740
use something, like the
embedding projector,

00:53:55.740 --> 00:53:59.520
depending on the type of
variables to do clustering.

00:53:59.520 --> 00:54:02.730
In other cases, I don't know
that we have a really good

00:54:02.730 --> 00:54:04.090
solution to offer.

00:54:04.090 --> 00:54:07.710
And, again, this is
exactly why I think you

00:54:07.710 --> 00:54:11.460
the audience have
power is that there's

00:54:11.460 --> 00:54:13.740
a wide opening for
you to actually make

00:54:13.740 --> 00:54:16.110
a big difference in the
field by providing the tools.

00:54:20.547 --> 00:54:25.340
HADLEY WICKHAM:
So Yim register asks, [INAUDIBLE]
is awesome for developers.

00:54:25.340 --> 00:54:27.410
But are you also
thinking about pathways

00:54:27.410 --> 00:54:30.000
for people affected
by these models

00:54:30.000 --> 00:54:31.880
so they can self-advocate
for models that

00:54:31.880 --> 00:54:34.050
about models that exclude them?

00:54:34.050 --> 00:54:34.790
FERNANDA VIEGAS:
Yeah.

00:54:37.830 --> 00:54:39.000
That's a very good question.

00:54:39.000 --> 00:54:40.910
I think it's a very hard one.

00:54:40.910 --> 00:54:43.430
One of the things
we're working on

00:54:43.430 --> 00:54:48.570
is just AI literacy, in general.

00:54:48.570 --> 00:54:52.210
So we are developing a number
of educational materials.

00:54:52.210 --> 00:54:54.860
The visualization
you saw with fairness

00:54:54.860 --> 00:54:58.250
was one of these moments
when we realized,

00:54:58.250 --> 00:55:01.850
oh, these simple visualizations
actually have a lot of power

00:55:01.850 --> 00:55:05.850
just to exactly that
start to educate people.

00:55:05.850 --> 00:55:11.270
We want a much broader set
of folks to be critical

00:55:11.270 --> 00:55:15.620
and to be engaged at a certain
level with this at a level

00:55:15.620 --> 00:55:18.980
where they feel they know
what questions to ask

00:55:18.980 --> 00:55:20.810
of the technology.

00:55:20.810 --> 00:55:24.035
And so all of this
stuff that we work on,

00:55:24.035 --> 00:55:28.220
we try to make it open source,
make it available publicly.

00:55:28.220 --> 00:55:33.540
But I also think that creating
tools that are code-free

00:55:33.540 --> 00:55:38.630
is extremely important
exactly to lower the barrier

00:55:38.630 --> 00:55:41.576
and to start to bridge
the communities.

00:55:41.576 --> 00:55:42.870
HADLEY WICKHAM:
Awesome.

00:55:42.870 --> 00:55:44.850
We also had kind
of a few questions,

00:55:44.850 --> 00:55:48.030
like, you're embedded inside
Google, which clearly, like,

00:55:48.030 --> 00:55:49.357
uses a bunch of algorithms.

00:55:49.357 --> 00:55:50.940
And sometimes those
outcomes are good.

00:55:50.940 --> 00:55:52.410
And sometimes they're bad.

00:55:52.410 --> 00:55:54.120
Like, how do you see
your role and kind

00:55:54.120 --> 00:55:57.960
of shifting how Google
uses algorithms for good?

00:55:57.960 --> 00:55:59.490
MARTIN WATTENBERG:
We like good algorithms.

00:55:59.490 --> 00:56:02.730
[LAUGHS] And I think,
more importantly, I think,

00:56:02.730 --> 00:56:06.010
in general, people want
to do the right thing.

00:56:06.010 --> 00:56:08.400
And I think one of the
things that's important

00:56:08.400 --> 00:56:13.620
is to give people tools to make
it sort of understandable what

00:56:13.620 --> 00:56:14.380
to do.

00:56:14.380 --> 00:56:19.080
One of the things that
is important, like, it

00:56:19.080 --> 00:56:20.550
can be very hard to know.

00:56:20.550 --> 00:56:23.190
Like, these things are
very complicated systems.

00:56:23.190 --> 00:56:27.510
And the more tools that we
have to help us figure out,

00:56:27.510 --> 00:56:30.610
OK, let's do this
rather than do that.

00:56:30.610 --> 00:56:33.190
How do we make decisions
around them the better?

00:56:33.190 --> 00:56:35.400
So we definitely view
ourselves as making tools

00:56:35.400 --> 00:56:37.060
both for Google.

00:56:37.060 --> 00:56:39.420
We open source that we want
I think for the whole world

00:56:39.420 --> 00:56:39.780
as well.

00:56:39.780 --> 00:56:40.640
HADLEY WICKHAM: Awesome.

00:56:40.640 --> 00:56:43.650
Thank you, I had another
personal question,

00:56:43.650 --> 00:56:45.930
which is, how long do
you spend coming up

00:56:45.930 --> 00:56:48.591
with, like, humorous and
good acronyms for your--

00:56:48.591 --> 00:56:49.091
[LAUGHS]

00:56:49.091 --> 00:56:51.977
[LAUGHS]

00:56:51.977 --> 00:56:53.146
FERNANDA VIEGAS:
I didn't notice.

00:56:53.146 --> 00:56:53.646
MARTIN WATTENBERG:
Yeah.

00:56:53.646 --> 00:56:54.250
[LAUGHS]

00:56:54.250 --> 00:56:55.605
You need to do more of that.

00:56:55.605 --> 00:56:56.105
Yeah.

00:56:56.105 --> 00:56:59.430
[LAUGHS]

00:56:59.430 --> 00:57:00.625
HADLEY WICKHAM:
Let's see.

00:57:03.780 --> 00:57:06.520
What else do we have?

00:57:06.520 --> 00:57:09.560
Have you created any examples
to illustrate the intuition

00:57:09.560 --> 00:57:12.375
behind adversarial images?

00:57:12.375 --> 00:57:14.600
Yeah.

00:57:14.600 --> 00:57:16.370
MARTIN WATTENBERG:
Between adversarial images?

00:57:16.370 --> 00:57:19.923
Oh, OK, so no.

00:57:19.923 --> 00:57:21.590
For a moment, I thought
you already said

00:57:21.590 --> 00:57:22.940
generative adversarial network.

00:57:22.940 --> 00:57:25.460
So we do have one of those--
something called GAN Lab.

00:57:25.460 --> 00:57:31.100
But adversarial images are
super interesting and weird.

00:57:31.100 --> 00:57:33.180
For those who may not
have heard of them,

00:57:33.180 --> 00:57:35.000
it's this phenomenon
that you can

00:57:35.000 --> 00:57:38.510
take an image that a computer
is correctly certain about.

00:57:38.510 --> 00:57:41.510
Like, it's a picture of a
cat or a frog or something.

00:57:41.510 --> 00:57:43.460
It for sure sees it as a cat.

00:57:43.460 --> 00:57:46.550
You change the pixels
in a tiny amount

00:57:46.550 --> 00:57:48.805
so that it's
imperceptible to a human.

00:57:48.805 --> 00:57:50.180
And all of a
sudden, the computer

00:57:50.180 --> 00:57:53.240
is certain it's like a
bathtub or something.

00:57:53.240 --> 00:57:55.520
And at first, it
was seen as like,

00:57:55.520 --> 00:57:58.063
some sort of weird one-off bug.

00:57:58.063 --> 00:57:59.480
But what's been
clear is that it's

00:57:59.480 --> 00:58:01.220
like a pervasive
thing that happens

00:58:01.220 --> 00:58:03.530
across lots of classifiers.

00:58:03.530 --> 00:58:05.690
I think one of the
reasons I don't

00:58:05.690 --> 00:58:07.910
think we've created something
that it sort of helps

00:58:07.910 --> 00:58:12.080
people understand it is that
the research community itself--

00:58:12.080 --> 00:58:14.570
I don't think there's
a crisp explanation.

00:58:14.570 --> 00:58:16.560
It's one of the
genuine mysteries.

00:58:16.560 --> 00:58:18.998
There are some interesting
ideas about maybe this

00:58:18.998 --> 00:58:21.290
has to do with the high
dimensional nature of the data.

00:58:21.290 --> 00:58:24.170
And any imperfect
classifier might have these.

00:58:24.170 --> 00:58:27.470
Or that, in fact, there
are important things

00:58:27.470 --> 00:58:29.940
that a computer picks up
on in terms of texture

00:58:29.940 --> 00:58:32.840
that the human eye
is just bad at.

00:58:32.840 --> 00:58:35.095
But I don't know that there's
a final answer to that.

00:58:35.095 --> 00:58:36.470
Therefore, it's
very hard to make

00:58:36.470 --> 00:58:38.360
an easy crisp
visualization of it.

00:58:38.360 --> 00:58:39.860
But it's a great
question, actually.

00:58:39.860 --> 00:58:41.690
It's something
probably we should try.

00:58:41.690 --> 00:58:42.440
HADLEY WICKHAM: Thanks.

00:58:42.440 --> 00:58:44.215
I think we'll do
one last question.

00:58:44.215 --> 00:58:45.590
So you've showed
a bunch of tools

00:58:45.590 --> 00:58:48.440
today that you can
use in your browser.

00:58:48.440 --> 00:58:50.200
Does the data ever
go to Google server?

00:58:50.200 --> 00:58:52.340
Or can you use it with
like, confidential data,

00:58:52.340 --> 00:58:54.590
like HIPAA-protected data?

00:58:54.590 --> 00:58:59.090
FERNANDA VIEGAS:
So anything that's
available on Google Cloud,

00:58:59.090 --> 00:59:01.250
you should be able to
use with your own data

00:59:01.250 --> 00:59:04.906
and confidentiality for sure.

00:59:04.906 --> 00:59:07.640
MARTIN WATTENBERG:
I mean, there is a
question about device--

00:59:07.640 --> 00:59:09.380
on device versus off device.

00:59:09.380 --> 00:59:13.280
And what I would say is rather
than giving a crisp answer

00:59:13.280 --> 00:59:15.530
to that up here, I would
read carefully the fine print

00:59:15.530 --> 00:59:16.340
of whichever tool.

00:59:16.340 --> 00:59:21.860
But many of these things do, in
fact, keep the data on device.

00:59:21.860 --> 00:59:24.410
FERNANDA VIEGAS:
There is a whole
other project we didn't

00:59:24.410 --> 00:59:26.000
get to talk about today here.

00:59:26.000 --> 00:59:30.560
But if you are
interested, tensorFlow.js

00:59:30.560 --> 00:59:33.950
Is it brings machine
learning to your browser.

00:59:33.950 --> 00:59:38.400
And one of the perks of
that, there is no install.

00:59:38.400 --> 00:59:39.260
There's no download.

00:59:39.260 --> 00:59:42.020
But one of the biggest
perks is privacy

00:59:42.020 --> 00:59:44.930
because it runs all locally.

00:59:44.930 --> 00:59:49.138
Nothing gets sent to any server.

00:59:49.138 --> 00:59:50.930
We didn't have time to
talk about it today.

00:59:50.930 --> 00:59:54.380
But it's definitely
worth checking out.

00:59:54.380 --> 00:59:55.210
HADLEY WICKHAM:
Awesome.

00:59:55.210 --> 00:59:55.710
Thank you.

00:59:55.710 --> 00:59:58.010
So please join me in
thanking Fernanda and Martin.

00:59:58.010 --> 01:00:01.360
[APPLAUSE]

